{"session_id":"svc-1778587990508068101","seq":0,"type":"session.start","ts":"2026-05-12T12:13:20.630175472Z","data":{"provider":"","model":"gpt-5.5","sticky":{"key_present":true,"bonus":0},"utilization":{"observed_at":"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z"},"requested_harness":"codex","resolved_harness":"codex","harness_source":"request_harness","requested_model":"gpt-5.5","resolved_model":"gpt-5.5","work_dir":"/var/tmp/ddx-exec-wt/.execute-bead-wt-fizeau-5b6512ef-20260512T121307-0cd30964","max_iterations":0,"prompt":"\u003cexecute-bead\u003e\n  \u003cinstructions\u003e\nYou are executing one bead inside an isolated DDx execution worktree. The bead\u0026#39;s \u0026lt;description\u0026gt; and \u0026lt;acceptance\u0026gt; are the completion contract — every AC must be provably satisfied by a specific code, test, or file you can point to after your commit.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## Step 0: size check\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;Too big if any holds:\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;- More than ~6 ACs spanning unrelated subsystems.\u0026#xA;- AC mixes design, implementation, integration tests, and docs as separate deliverables.\u0026#xA;- Description names multiple feature-sized sub-pieces.\u0026#xA;- More than ~500 lines across ~5+ files in unrelated packages.\u0026#xA;- If the bead description exceeds 8000 bytes, treat Step 0 as a split-first pass.\u0026#xA;- Auto-decomposition is capped at depth 2: root beads may split once, decomposed children once more; third-level splits must be rejected with an explanation.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;If too big, decompose — do not attempt the work:\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;1. `ddx bead create` for each child slice (copy parent\u0026#39;s labels and spec-id).\u0026#xA;2. `ddx bead dep add \u0026lt;child-id\u0026gt; \u0026lt;parent-id\u0026gt;` to record edges.\u0026#xA;3. `ddx bead update \u0026lt;parent-id\u0026gt; --notes \u0026#39;decomposed into \u0026lt;child-ids\u0026gt;\u0026#39;`.\u0026#xA;4. Write `.ddx/executions/20260512T121307-0cd30964/no_changes_rationale.txt` listing child IDs, then stop.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;A clean decomposition is a success. Do not mix implementation with decomposition.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## How to work\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;- Read first. If the bead names files, specs, or prior beads, read them before editing — do not guess.\u0026#xA;- Cross-reference each AC against concrete evidence (test name, file path, function) before committing. If you cannot point at it, it is not done.\u0026#xA;- Run the project\u0026#39;s test and lint commands before committing. **Do not commit red code** — fix failures first.\u0026#xA;- Stage with `git add \u0026lt;specific-paths\u0026gt;`; never `git add -A` (the worktree may have unrelated WIP).\u0026#xA;- Commit exactly once when green; conventional-commit subject ending with `[\u0026lt;bead-id\u0026gt;]`. Stop after the commit.\u0026#xA;- Do not modify files outside the bead\u0026#39;s scope.\u0026#xA;- If you cannot finish, write `.ddx/executions/20260512T121307-0cd30964/no_changes_rationale.txt` (what is done, what blocks, what a follow-up needs) before exiting. No commit and no rationale ⇒ DDx records `no_evidence_produced`. A well-justified no_changes beats a bad commit.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## no_changes contract\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;`.ddx/executions/20260512T121307-0cd30964/no_changes_rationale.txt` must contain one:\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;- `verification_command: \u0026lt;cmd\u0026gt;` — repo cwd; exit 0 closes, nonzero rejects.\u0026#xA;- `status: open` + `reason: \u0026lt;retryable\u0026gt;` — open, smart retry.\u0026#xA;- `status: proposed` + `reason: \u0026lt;operator needed\u0026gt;` — operator lane.\u0026#xA;- `status: blocked` + `reason: \u0026lt;external blocker\u0026gt;` — blocked lane.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;No pseudo-statuses. Bare rationales and `status: needs_investigation` reject.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;To request orchestrator decomposition, add `orchestrator_action: decompose` alongside `status: open`.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## Reports\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;Freestanding artifacts (investigation reports, findings docs) go under `.ddx/executions/20260512T121307-0cd30964/` (the per-attempt evidence directory under `.ddx/executions/`). **Never write reports to `/tmp` or any path outside the repository** — out-of-repo paths are invisible to the post-merge reviewer and cause BLOCK on missing evidence. If the bead names a specific in-repo path, use it; else default to `.ddx/executions/20260512T121307-0cd30964/\u0026lt;short-name\u0026gt;.md`. Stage and commit the report alongside code.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## Mode and overrides\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;DDX_MODE=bead_execution: edit code/docs for bead AC. Only broad queue-steward default is overridden; tracker, merge-policy, verification, safety stay active. Bead description/AC override CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, and project defaults.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## Review gate\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;- The review is a gate, not an escape hatch — meet every AC in this pass.\u0026#xA;- Address every BLOCKING `\u0026lt;review-findings\u0026gt;` item; do not declare `no_changes` with blocking findings open.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## Constraints\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;- Work only inside this execution worktree.\u0026#xA;- Keep `.ddx/executions/` intact — DDx uses it as execution evidence.\u0026#xA;- **Never run `ddx init`** — the workspace is initialized; running it corrupts the bead queue.\u0026#xA;- Do not modify files outside the bead\u0026#39;s named scope.\u0026#xA;- Do not rewrite CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, or other project-instructions files unless the bead asks.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## When the work is done\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;After the commit succeeds and every AC is verified, stop. Return control to the orchestrator — do not keep exploring or testing.\n  \u003c/instructions\u003e\n  \u003cbead id=\"fizeau-5b6512ef\"\u003e\n    \u003ctitle\u003eADR-011: Cost-based routing with quota-pool-aware fallback\u003c/title\u003e\n    \u003cdescription\u003e\nDesign ADR (no implementation in this bead) for cost-based routing\u0026#xA;where quota-remaining is a factor in the cost score. Implementation\u0026#xA;follows in a separate EPIC after the ADR is accepted.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;Per operator direction 2026-05-11:\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;\u0026gt; For a given model power, we need to intelligently choose based on\u0026#xA;\u0026gt; \u0026#34;cost\u0026#34;, where remaining usage quota impacts the cost.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;Cost model (proposed for ADR-011):\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;- **Subscription with quota remaining**: effective cost ≈ 0 (or\u0026#xA;  quota-fraction-aware: lower-cost the more quota remains).\u0026#xA;- **Subscription with quota exhausted**: drop from candidates.\u0026#xA;- **Per-token API**: effective cost = catalog `cost_input_per_m` ×\u0026#xA;  estimated input tokens + `cost_output_per_m` × estimated output\u0026#xA;  tokens.\u0026#xA;- **Local provider** (free): effective cost = 0 if eligible (per bead\u0026#xA;  D\u0026#39;s IncludeByDefault filter), else dropped.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;Quota signal sources:\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;- HTTP rate-limit response headers (per recent commit 7776890e —\u0026#xA;  `quota_exhausted` already plumbed).\u0026#xA;- Subscription `/usage` endpoints when available.\u0026#xA;- In-memory recent-rejection rate as fallback.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;Quota-pool semantic (from bead E1):\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;- Models declare a `quota_pool` (defaulting to provider system).\u0026#xA;- Pool exhaustion drops all models in that pool from the candidate set.\u0026#xA;- Models in a different pool (e.g., `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` in\u0026#xA;  `openai-codex-spark`) become eligible as fallbacks even if not\u0026#xA;  version-newest, satisfying the \u0026#34;use spark when main pool is empty\u0026#34;\u0026#xA;  operator request.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;Power-floor semantic:\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;- Caller / DDx specifies required `MinPower` (and optional `MaxPower`).\u0026#xA;- Routing filters candidates to `MinPower ≤ candidate.power ≤ MaxPower`.\u0026#xA;- Among survivors, pick min effective cost.\u0026#xA;- Tie-break: prefer subscription over PAYG; prefer lower-power if it\u0026#xA;  exceeds requirement (cheapest qualified candidate, not most powerful).\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## ADR scope\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;The ADR document itself, written to\u0026#xA;`docs/helix/02-design/adr/ADR-011-cost-based-routing-with-quota-pools.md`.\u0026#xA;Standard ADR structure: Context, Decision, Consequences, Out of scope,\u0026#xA;References.\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## Decision points the ADR must resolve\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;1. Quota fraction → cost mapping (linear? step function? thresholds?).\u0026#xA;2. Quota window time handling (do we reserve quota for later? probably\u0026#xA;   not in v1 — use available now).\u0026#xA;3. Token estimate source (the request itself? a heuristic? a learned\u0026#xA;   model?). v1 likely uses a coarse system-prompt-length-based\u0026#xA;   estimate.\u0026#xA;4. Fallback chain ordering when quota exhausts mid-request (single try?\u0026#xA;   retry on alternate?).\u0026#xA;5. How `IncludeByDefault: false` (bead D) composes with cost ranking\u0026#xA;   (drops from default candidate set entirely; not just deprioritized).\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## In-scope files\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;- docs/helix/02-design/adr/ADR-011-cost-based-routing-with-quota-pools.md\u0026#xA;  (new)\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;## Out of scope\u0026#xA;\u0026#xA;- Implementation (separate EPIC after ADR acceptance).\u0026#xA;- Quota signal infrastructure beyond what 7776890e already provides\u0026#xA;  (any new signals needed get filed as their own beads under the EPIC).\u0026#xA;- Per-tenant or per-key quota tracking (flag as future scope in the\u0026#xA;  ADR).\n    \u003c/description\u003e\n    \u003cacceptance\u003e\n1. `test -f docs/helix/02-design/adr/ADR-011-cost-based-routing-with-quota-pools.md` passes and `rg -n \u0026#34;^## (Context|Decision|Consequences|Out of scope|References)\u0026#34; docs/helix/02-design/adr/ADR-011-cost-based-routing-with-quota-pools.md` finds all five required ADR sections.\u0026#xA;2. The ADR text explicitly covers all five decision points; verify with `rg -n \u0026#34;quota fraction|quota window|token estimate|fallback chain|IncludeByDefault\u0026#34; docs/helix/02-design/adr/ADR-011-cost-based-routing-with-quota-pools.md`.\u0026#xA;3. The ADR references ADR-005, ADR-006, ADR-007, fizeau-c04be6b0, and fizeau-d18e11f5; verify with `rg -n \u0026#34;ADR-005|ADR-006|ADR-007|fizeau-c04be6b0|fizeau-d18e11f5\u0026#34; docs/helix/02-design/adr/ADR-011-cost-based-routing-with-quota-pools.md`.\u0026#xA;4. The bead commit contains the ADR document and no implementation files; verify with `git show --name-only --format= HEAD` after the worker commit.\n    \u003c/acceptance\u003e\n    \u003cnotes\u003e\nOperator refined ACs into mechanical file/rg/git checks and cleared stale AC-quality gate metadata.\n    \u003c/notes\u003e\n    \u003clabels\u003earea:routing, area:adr, kind:design\u003c/labels\u003e\n    \u003cmetadata spec-id=\"ADR-005\" base-rev=\"57988c094dafb2f5a75df72e125e445887eaa3d9\" bundle=\".ddx/executions/20260512T121307-0cd30964\"/\u003e\n  \u003c/bead\u003e\n  \u003cgoverning\u003e\n    \u003cref id=\"ADR-005\" path=\"docs/helix/02-design/adr/ADR-005-smart-routing-replaces-model-routes.md\"\u003eADR-005: Power-Based Routing Replaces `model_routes`\u003c/ref\u003e\n  \u003c/governing\u003e\n\u003c/execute-bead\u003e\n","metadata":{"DDX_MODE":"bead_execution","attempt_id":"20260512T121307-0cd30964","base_rev":"57988c094dafb2f5a75df72e125e445887eaa3d9","bead_id":"fizeau-5b6512ef","bundle_path":".ddx/executions/20260512T121307-0cd30964","correlation_id":"fizeau-5b6512ef:20260512T121307-0cd30964","prompt_file":".ddx/executions/20260512T121307-0cd30964/prompt.md","prompt_sha":"afa96f1bb9c2d5fe6260782b3cf16b421c4d0120d894ac9508bc59d85b630da2","role":"implementer","session_id":"eb-382df93b","worker_id":""}}}
